{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2585","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2585","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2585\",\"parent_revision\":\"ad10c4c15bcc8f972fdba6d5583022a64666cc3e657694e471d88d8b74193fdd\",\"root_revision\":\"ad10c4c15bcc8f972fdba6d5583022a64666cc3e657694e471d88d8b74193fdd\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nFix A (_sections_from_body_or_yaml, merged 2026-06-04) protects items that have a local YAML file by falling back to YAML-sourced sections when the GitHub body contains no entry-block wrappers. It does not protect items that exist only on GitHub with no local YAML cache.\\n\\nThe root cause was not corrected in the write path: sync_groomed_to_github_issue (gh_client.py) still writes plain-text section content to the GitHub issue body without the div/sub timestamp wrapper format that parse_entries expects. For GitHub-only items, _sections_from_body_or_yaml has no YAML item to fall back to, and re-parsing the plain-text body still produces 0000-00-00T00:00:00Z for every entry.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: Fix requires writing entry-block wrappers (div sub timestamp format) in sync_groomed_to_github_issue so round-tripping through GitHub preserves real entry IDs. Needs a test that validates the GitHub body round-trip path \u2014 Fix A's tests mock view_enrich_from_github directly and do not cover this case. Relevant file: backlog_core/gh_client.py, function sync_groomed_to_github_issue.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Claim 1**: sync_groomed_to_github_issue in gh_client.py writes plain-text section content without entry-block wrappers.\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: sync_groomed_to_github_issue / _insert_named_section writes plain content with no `<div><sub>` wrappers\\nevidence: Executed `_insert_named_section(body, 'Acceptance Criteria', 'My AC item', '2026-06-05')` \u2014 result has zero ENTRY_RE matches. Imports in gh_client.py (lines 12\u201351) confirm no import of `entry_blocks` or `wrap_entry`.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py lines 1726\u20131766 (_insert_named_section); manual execution 2026-06-05\\n\\n**Claim 2**: ENTRY_RE.search(wrap_entry(content)) returns True \u2014 wrap_entry output is round-trip parseable.\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: wrap_entry produces output that ENTRY_RE will match\\nevidence: `wrap_entry('test content')` returns `'<div><sub>2026-06-05T03:03:48.805309Z</sub>\\\\n\\\\ntest content\\\\n</div>'`; `bool(ENTRY_RE.search(s))` returns `True`\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/entry_blocks.py lines 11 (ENTRY_RE), 39\u201345 (wrap_entry); executed 2026-06-05\\n\\n**Claim 3**: _sections_from_body_or_yaml (Fix A) protects YAML-backed items but NOT GitHub-only items.\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Fix A falls back to YAML item when body has no ENTRY_RE match; GitHub-only items have no YAML item to fall back to\\nevidence: Read operations.py lines 3185\u20133215: `if ENTRY_RE.search(body): ...` else `if item and item.sections: ...` else `{}`. GitHub-only items have `item=None` so the third branch returns `{}`.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py lines 3185\u20133215; read 2026-06-05\\n\\n**Claim 4**: Test file for Fix A (test_entry_id_zero_timestamp_bug.py) mocks view_enrich_from_github \u2014 does not test the GitHub write path.\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Existing tests for the zero-timestamp bug do not cover the sync_groomed_to_github_issue write path\\nevidence: test_entry_id_zero_timestamp_bug.py uses `mocker.patch(\\\"backlog_core.operations.view_enrich_from_github\\\")` \u2014 this tests the view/read path. No test in the test suite uses `sync_groomed_to_github_issue` in a round-trip scenario.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_entry_id_zero_timestamp_bug.py lines 126\u2013193; grep for test_sync_groomed/test_github_round_trip returned no results; 2026-06-05\\n\\n**Claim 5**: memory_backend and sqlite_backend also write plain content (same bug class).\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Both non-GitHub backends write groomed_content without entry-block wrappers\\nevidence: memory_backend.py line 423: `issue[\\\"body\\\"] = f\\\"{issue['body']}\\\\n\\\\n## {heading}\\\\n\\\\n{groomed_content}\\\"`. sqlite_backend.py line 811: `new_body = f\\\"{current}\\\\n\\\\n## {heading}\\\\n\\\\n{groomed_content}\\\"`. Neither calls wrap_entry.\\nsource: backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py line 423; backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py line 811; read 2026-06-05\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:3b7b41cb1bf1b11e755d2da4534b7fde -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: fix(backlog_core): sync_groomed_to_github_issue must write entry-block wrappers so GitHub-only items preserve entry IDs\\nDate: 2026-06-04\\nGoal: Fix the write path in sync_groomed_to_github_issue to emit entry-block wrappers so that GitHub-only items round-trip without losing entry IDs.\\nConditions:\\n1. Root function to fix is sync_groomed_to_github_issue in gh_client.py | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Read gh_client.py lines 1769\u20131803\\n2. Entry-block wrapper format (ENTRY_RE, wrap_entry) defined in entry_blocks.py | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Read entry_blocks.py lines 11, 39\u201345\\n3. Fix A (_sections_from_body_or_yaml) is in operations.py \u2014 protects YAML-backed items only | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Read operations.py lines 3185\u20133215\\n4. Test file for Fix A exists: tests/test_entry_id_zero_timestamp_bug.py | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Read test file lines 126\u2013193\\n5. Test for Fix B (GitHub round-trip for GitHub-only items) does not exist yet | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: grep for test_sync_groomed/test_entry_block_roundtrip returned no results\\n6. _insert_named_section in gh_client.py writes plain content without wrap_entry | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Executed _insert_named_section \u2014 ENTRY_RE.search(result) = False\\n7. backend_protocol.BackendProtocol.sync_groomed_to_github_issue declares the interface | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: grep backend_protocol.py line 643\\n8. memory_backend and sqlite_backend also implement sync_groomed_to_github_issue | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Read memory_backend.py line 423, sqlite_backend.py line 811 \u2014 both confirmed writing plain content\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 8: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by fact-checker \u2014 cite: read memory_backend.py:423 and sqlite_backend.py:811)\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **fix(backlog_core): sync_groomed_to_github_issue must write entry-block wrappers so github-only items preserve entry ids** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation 2026-06-04 \u2014 concerns block from python-cli-architect during Fix A implementation\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-05\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-05)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n**Steps to reproduce**:\\n1. Create or identify a backlog item that has no local YAML cache (GitHub-only \u2014 item exists only as a GitHub Issue, not in `~/.dh/projects/.../backlog/*.yaml`).\\n2. Call `backlog_groom(selector='#N', section='Acceptance Criteria', content='some criterion')`.\\n3. Wait for sync to GitHub to complete.\\n4. Call `backlog_view(selector='#N', summary=False, section='Acceptance Criteria')`.\\n5. Observe `sections[\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\"][\\\"entries\\\"][0][\\\"id\\\"]` \u2014 it is `\\\"0000-00-00T00:00:00Z\\\"` regardless of when the content was written.\\n\\n**Trigger condition**: item must be GitHub-only (no YAML file in local backlog cache). Items with a local YAML file are protected by Fix A and will show real IDs.\\n\\n**Verified**: via source code inspection \u2014 `_insert_named_section` (gh_client.py line 1726) writes plain text; `ENTRY_RE.search(result)` returns False.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n**Priority**: P2 \u2014 workflow correctness defect. Affects any GitHub-only item that is groomed and then re-read. Entry IDs are used for edit/strike operations; zero IDs silently break entry targeting.\\n\\n**Effort**: S \u2014 single-function fix. Add `from .entry_blocks import wrap_entry` to gh_client.py imports. In `_insert_named_section`, wrap `content` with `wrap_entry(content)` before inserting. Add one test. Estimated: 1\u20132 hours including test.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] `_insert_named_section` wraps the section `content` argument with `wrap_entry()` before inserting into the GitHub body\\n- [ ] A GitHub-only item round-trip test: write content via `_insert_named_section` \u2192 parse the result with `parse_entries` \u2192 entry ID is a valid ISO timestamp, not `0000-00-00T00:00:00Z`\\n- [ ] Existing tests in `test_entry_id_zero_timestamp_bug.py` continue to pass (Fix A remains intact)\\n- [ ] `gh_client.py` imports `wrap_entry` from `.entry_blocks`\\n- [ ] `uv run pytest plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/` passes with no regressions\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n- `entry_blocks.py::wrap_entry` (line 39) \u2014 the function to import and use\\n- `entry_blocks.py::ENTRY_RE` (line 11) \u2014 the regex that validates round-trip parseability\\n- `entry_blocks.py::parse_entries` (line 133) \u2014 use in test to verify round-trip\\n- Fix A reference: `operations.py::_sections_from_body_or_yaml` (line 3185) \u2014 context for why Fix B is needed\\n- `tests/test_entry_id_zero_timestamp_bug.py` \u2014 reference test patterns (M1/M2 structure)\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n**Primary change**:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py` \u2014 add `wrap_entry` import; modify `_insert_named_section` to wrap content\\n\\n**New test file**:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_sync_groomed_entry_block_roundtrip.py` \u2014 test that content written through `_insert_named_section` / `sync_groomed_to_github_issue` is parseable by `parse_entries` with real entry IDs\\n\\n**Existing test file (must stay green)**:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_entry_id_zero_timestamp_bug.py`\\n\\n**Secondary consideration** (out of scope for this fix, separate backlog item):\\n- `backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py` \u2014 also writes plain content; not a live GitHub path, lower priority\\n- `backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py` \u2014 same\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Fix A (`_sections_from_body_or_yaml`, merged 2026-06-04) \u2014 no dependency; Fix B is a parallel correction to the write path. Fix A remains in place as a defense for YAML-backed items.\\n- No other open items block this fix.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n**Estimate**: S (small)\\n- Code change: ~5 lines (1 import, 1 function call wrapping in `_insert_named_section`)\\n- Test: ~40 lines (one test class, two test methods \u2014 write path and round-trip assertion)\\n- Total: 1\u20132 hours including quality gates\\n\\n**Risk**: Low. `wrap_entry` produces deterministic output. The only behavioral change is that the written GitHub body now contains HTML entry-block wrappers, which is the intended format. Existing parsers (`parse_entries`, `ENTRY_RE`) already handle this format correctly.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\n- None identified.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n- `backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py::sync_groomed_to_github_issue` \u2014 delegates directly to `gh_client.sync_groomed_to_github_issue`; no change needed, fix is upstream\\n- `backlog_core/operations.py::groom_item` (line 1087) \u2014 calls `sync_groomed_to_github_issue` to write each groomed section; benefits from fix automatically\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py::sync_groomed_to_github_issue` (line 423) \u2014 writes plain `groomed_content` without wrappers; if memory backend is used in tests as a stand-in for round-trip behavior, needs parallel fix\\n- `backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py::sync_groomed_to_github_issue` (line 811) \u2014 same pattern: plain `groomed_content` appended without wrappers\\n- `backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py::sync_groomed_to_github_issue` \u2014 raises `NotImplementedError`; not affected\\n- `backlog_core/backend_protocol.py` \u2014 declares the `sync_groomed_to_github_issue` interface; no change needed\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py::sync_groomed_to_github_issue` \u2014 writes groomed content to GitHub issue body without entry-block wrappers; fix: wrap each section's content with `wrap_entry()` from `entry_blocks.py` before writing\\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py::_insert_named_section` \u2014 inserts named section content as plain text; fix: wrap `content` argument with `wrap_entry()` before inserting\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- None identified.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: DIVERGENT\\n\\n**Design intent**: The entry-block format (`<div><sub>timestamp</sub>\u2026</div>`) is the canonical wire format for backlog section entries, defined in `entry_blocks.py`. The contract is that any body written to GitHub must use this format so that `parse_entries` / `ENTRY_RE` can recover real timestamps on re-read.\\n\\n**Current implementation**: `sync_groomed_to_github_issue` \u2192 `_insert_named_section` writes raw plain text. This violates the contract: the write path and the read path use incompatible formats.\\n\\n**Divergence**: Fix A (operations.py `_sections_from_body_or_yaml`) added a YAML fallback to compensate for the broken write path. This is a symptom treatment, not a root fix. The design intent requires the write path itself to emit entry-block wrappers.\\n\\n**Impact of divergence**: GitHub-only items (no local YAML cache) cannot benefit from the YAML fallback and continue to produce zero-timestamp IDs.\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- None identified.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated to new interface\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every agent instruction updated\\n- [ ] Old interface deprecated or removed (if replacing)\\n- [ ] CI/config files updated and validated\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n**Rationale**: A traceable failure with an identifiable cause chain: Fix A corrected the read path (_sections_from_body_or_yaml) but the write path (sync_groomed_to_github_issue \u2192 _insert_named_section) was never fixed. The write path produces plain-text bodies; the read path expects entry-block wrappers. For GitHub-only items, there is no YAML fallback, so every re-read of the GitHub body produces zero-timestamp IDs. The cause chain is complete and deterministic.\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys\\n**Scenario Target**: developer grooms a GitHub-only backlog item \u2192 re-reads it \u2192 all entry IDs show 0000-00-00T00:00:00Z \u2192 scenario improvement: entry IDs are preserved through the GitHub write/read round-trip\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys\\n\\n**Why 1**: All backlog item entry IDs show 0000-00-00T00:00:00Z for GitHub-only items after grooming.\\n\u2192 Because _sections_from_body_or_yaml falls back to `{}` when the GitHub body has no ENTRY_RE matches and there is no local YAML item.\\n\\n**Why 2**: The GitHub body has no ENTRY_RE matches.\\n\u2192 Because sync_groomed_to_github_issue / _insert_named_section writes section content as plain text without wrapping it in `<div><sub>timestamp</sub>\u2026</div>` entry-block format.\\n\\n**Why 3**: _insert_named_section writes plain text.\\n\u2192 Because gh_client.py does not import `entry_blocks` and no entry-block wrapping was implemented in the original gh_client write path.\\n\\n**Why 4**: The entry-block format was not applied in the write path when Fix A was implemented.\\n\u2192 Fix A addressed only the read path (adding _sections_from_body_or_yaml with YAML fallback). The write path (gh_client.py::_insert_named_section) was identified as a concern but not corrected in the same PR.\\n\\n**Why 5**: The write path omission was not caught by tests.\\n\u2192 Fix A's tests mock view_enrich_from_github at the operations layer. They never call sync_groomed_to_github_issue, so the fact that the written body lacks entry-block wrappers was invisible to the test suite.\\n\\n**Root cause**: gh_client.py::_insert_named_section writes plain-text section content. A targeted fix adds `from .entry_blocks import wrap_entry` to gh_client.py and wraps `content` with `wrap_entry(content)` in `_insert_named_section` before inserting. A companion test validates that content written through sync_groomed_to_github_issue \u2192 re-read via parse_entries returns a real (non-zero) timestamp ID.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `gh_client.py::sync_groomed_to_github_issue` \u2014 primary write path; **root fix location**\\n- `gh_client.py::_insert_named_section` \u2014 called by above; writes section content as plain string\\n- `entry_blocks.py::wrap_entry` \u2014 produces `<div><sub>{timestamp}</sub>\\\\n\\\\n{content}\\\\n</div>`; must be imported and used in gh_client.py\\n- `entry_blocks.py::ENTRY_RE` \u2014 the regex that `_sections_from_body_or_yaml` uses to detect wrappers; the fix makes the written body parseable by this pattern\\n- `operations.py::_sections_from_body_or_yaml` \u2014 Fix A fallback; works correctly for YAML-backed items; remains in place post-Fix B\\n- `tests/test_entry_id_zero_timestamp_bug.py` \u2014 Fix A tests; mock `view_enrich_from_github` directly, do not cover GitHub body write path\\n- `backends/memory_backend.py`, `sqlite_backend.py` \u2014 also write plain content; may need same wrapper fix for non-GitHub backends\\n\",\"digest\":\"41b30f41f29f88a98c2bb730ffecce09fb953c156585fc4000eb7cfcbd3be84b\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCMR3A\"}"}